/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*---------------------------- File search.dox -----------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_search */
/** \defgroup theory_search Theory of searching
 \ingroup area_search

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/search` answer questions on a vector by
 looking at few of its items: the place of a key in a sorted vector (the binary
 search of section 1.2 of the book and the search by interpolation of section
 12.1), the minimum and the local optima, the item of a given rank (the
 selection of section 17.1), and the minimum of any range of an array after a
 preprocessing, together with the engineered versions of these searches that
 count the misses of the cache and the branches that the processor mispredicts.
 This page collects what the headers take for granted: the definitions and the
 notation shared by all of them (see \ref theory_search_notation), the lower
 bounds and the invariants of the searches in a sorted vector (see \ref
 theory_search_sorted), the searches laid out for the memory (see \ref
 theory_search_memory), the minimum and the local optima (see \ref
 theory_search_minimum), the selection (see \ref theory_search_select) and the
 range minima (see \ref theory_search_rmq). The pseudocode of each algorithm is
 instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together
 with the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_search_notation Definitions and notation

 The input is a vector \f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$ of items of a totally ordered
 universe, compared by an order `less()` (the operator \f$ < \f$ unless one is
 given); \f$ A \f$ is <em>sorted</em> if \f$ A[ i ] \le A[ i + 1 ] \f$ for
 every \f$ i \f$. A <em>comparison</em> is the evaluation of the order on two
 items, and the cost of a search is the number of its comparisons, the other
 operations being proportional to it, unless the comment of a function says
 otherwise. The <em>rank</em> of an item is its place in the sorted order, from
 1 in `Selection.h` as in the book and from 0 in `NthElement.h` and
 `SortedSearches.h` as in the standard library.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ A[ i .. j ] \f$, \f$ A[ i .. j ) \f$</td>
     <td>the items of the places from \f$ i \f$ to \f$ j \f$, with
         \f$ j \f$ and without it</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{lo} \f$, \f$ \mathit{hi} \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>the ends of the part still to examine and its middle place,
         i.e., \f$ m = \mathit{lo} + \lfloor \Delta / 2 \rfloor \f$ with
         \f$ \Delta = \mathit{hi} - \mathit{lo} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{lowerBound}( v ) \f$</td>
     <td>the first place of an item not smaller than \f$ v \f$, \f$ n \f$ if
         none; \f$ \mathrm{upperBound}( v ) \f$ the first place of an item
         larger than \f$ v \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{RMQ}( i , j ) \f$</td>
     <td>the place of the leftmost minimum of \f$ A[ i .. j ] \f$,
         \f$ i \le j \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ B \f$</td>
     <td>the items in a line of cache of 64 bytes, e.g., 16 of 32 bits</td>
     </tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \epsilon \f$, \f$ \epsilon_r \f$</td>
     <td>the largest error, in places, of the lines of a learned index at
         its last level and at the others</td></tr>
 </table>

 A comparison-based search is a <em>decision tree</em> (section 2.7.4): a
 binary tree whose inner nodes are the comparisons and whose leaves are the
 answers, a run being the path from the root to a leaf, so that the worst case
 of the search is the height of its tree (`decisionTree()` of `BinarySearch.h`
 builds the tree of the binary search).

 \section theory_search_sorted Searches in a sorted vector

 <em>Lemma 1 (the lower bound of the search).</em> Every comparison-based
 search for the place \f$ \mathrm{lowerBound}( v ) \f$ in a sorted vector of
 \f$ n \f$ items makes, in the worst case,
 \f[
   \lceil \log_2 ( n + 1 ) \rceil \tag{1}
 \f]
 comparisons at least.

 Indeed, the answer is one of the \f$ n + 1 \f$ places \f$ 0 , \ldots , n \f$,
 each of which is the answer for some \f$ v \f$, and its decision tree has
 therefore \f$ n + 1 \f$ leaves at least; a binary tree of height \f$ h \f$ has
 at most \f$ 2^h \f$ of them, whence (1).

 The binary search meets (1). `lowerBound()` keeps the invariant
 \f[
   A[ \mathit{lo} - 1 ] < v \le A[ \mathit{hi} ] , \tag{2}
 \f]
 with \f$ A[ -1 ] = -\infty \f$ and \f$ A[ n ] = +\infty \f$, which holds at
 the start with \f$ \mathit{lo} = 0 \f$ and \f$ \mathit{hi} = n \f$; a
 comparison of \f$ v \f$ with \f$ A[ m ] \f$ moves one of the two ends to
 \f$ m \f$ or to \f$ m + 1 \f$ and keeps (2), and the length
 \f$ \mathit{hi} - \mathit{lo} \f$ goes from \f$ \ell \f$ to at most
 \f$ \lfloor \ell / 2 \rfloor \f$; hence the loop ends after
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 ( n + 1 ) \rceil \f$ comparisons with
 \f$ \mathit{lo} = \mathit{hi} \f$, which is the answer by (2). The binary
 search of the book (`binarySearch()`) stops as soon as it meets \f$ v \f$, at
 the price of two comparisons per step, and its decision tree has all its
 levels full but the last one, so that its height is
 \f$ \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor \f$. The middle is computed as
 \f$ \mathit{lo} + \lfloor ( \mathit{hi} - \mathit{lo} ) / 2 \rfloor \f$, which
 never overflows, unlike \f$ ( \mathit{lo} + \mathit{hi} ) / 2 \f$.

 When the answer \f$ h \f$ is near the start, or the length of the vector is
 not known, the search of Bentley and Yao doubles a bound before the binary
 search (`gallopSearch()` of `BinarySearch.h`, `exponentialSearch()` and
 `unboundedSearch()` of `MonotoneSearch.h`): the probes at the places
 \f$ 0 , 1 , 3 , 7 , \ldots , 2^k - 1 \f$ stop at the first
 \f$ k = \lceil \log_2 ( h + 1 ) \rceil \f$ with \f$ A[ 2^k - 1 ] \ge v \f$,
 and a binary search on the last interval, of length \f$ 2^{k-1} \f$, costs
 \f$ k - 1 \f$ more; in all
 \f[
   2 \lceil \log_2 ( h + 1 ) \rceil + O( 1 ) \tag{3}
 \f]
 comparisons, which depend on the answer and not on \f$ n \f$. Bentley and Yao
 also show that applying the same idea to the search of \f$ k \f$ gives
 \f$ \log_2 h + \log_2 \log_2 h + \cdots \f$, close to the lower bound
 \f$ \log_2 h \f$. The same scheme searches any monotone predicate on the
 integers, e.g., the integer square root (`integerSqrt()`).

 The search by interpolation of section 12.1 (`interpolationSearch()` of
 `InterpolationSearch.h`) probes the place that a linear model of the keys
 predicts,
 \f[
   m = \mathit{lo} + \Big\lfloor ( \mathit{hi} - \mathit{lo} )
       \frac{v - A[ \mathit{lo} ]}{A[ \mathit{hi} ] - A[ \mathit{lo} ]}
       \Big\rfloor , \tag{4}
 \f]
 instead of the middle one. On keys drawn independently from the uniform
 distribution the expected number of probes is \f$ \log_2 \log_2 n + O( 1 ) \f$
 (Perl, Itai and Avni), since the expected error of (4) on a part of length
 \f$ \ell \f$ is of the order of \f$ \sqrt{\ell} \f$, so that each probe takes,
 in expectation, the square root of the length; on keys far from uniform (e.g.,
 growing exponentially) a probe may exclude a single item, and the worst case
 is \f$ O( n ) \f$. The learned index refines the same idea with a model that
 has a guaranteed error (see \ref theory_search_memory).

 Two searches of Knuth trade the division of the middle for additions alone:
 the Fibonacci search (`fibonacciSearch()` of `KnuthSearches.h`) splits a part
 of length \f$ F_{k+1} - 1 \f$ at its \f$ F_k \f$-th item into parts of lengths
 \f$ F_k - 1 \f$ and \f$ F_{k-1} - 1 \f$, at most about
 \f$ \log_\phi n \approx 1.44 \log_2 n \f$ comparisons with \f$ \phi \f$ the
 golden ratio, and the uniform binary search (`uniformBinarySearch()`) keeps a
 place and the width of the next step, taken from a table computed once.
 Finally, the searches of `SortedSearches.h` apply the invariant (2) to other
 predicates that are false and then true along the vector: the rotation point
 of a rotated vector, which is the first place with
 \f$ A[ m ] \le A[ n - 1 ] \f$; the item closest to a value, which is
 \f$ A[ r ] \f$ or \f$ A[ r - 1 ] \f$ for \f$ r = \mathrm{lowerBound}( x ) \f$;
 and the item of rank \f$ k \f$ of two sorted vectors, where the number
 \f$ i \f$ of the \f$ k + 1 \f$ smallest items taken from the shorter vector is
 the first one with \f$ S[ i ] \ge L[ k - i ] \f$.

