 \section theory_search_memory Searches laid out for the memory

 On a modern processor the comparisons of a binary search cost less than the
 two events that surround them: a miss of the cache at each of the first
 levels, since the places probed are far apart, and a misprediction of the
 branch that follows each comparison, which on random keys is right half of the
 times. The headers `StaticSearch.h` and `PGMIndex.h` remove the two costs, one
 at a time.

 The binary search with no branch of Khuong and Morin
 (`branchlessLowerBound()`) keeps a base \f$ b \f$ and a length \f$ \ell \f$,
 and at each step sets \f$ b \gets b + h \f$ if \f$ A[ b + h ] < v \f$, with
 \f$ h = \lfloor \ell / 2 \rfloor \f$, and \f$ \ell \gets \ell - h \f$ whatever
 the outcome: the comparison feeds a conditional move rather than a jump, and
 since the next two places that may be probed are known, both are prefetched,
 so that the misses of two steps overlap. The S+ tree of Slotin (`SPlusTree`)
 removes the misses instead: the sorted items, cut into nodes of \f$ B \f$
 items, are the leaves of a static B+-tree whose inner nodes have \f$ B \f$
 keys and \f$ B + 1 \f$ children, stored level by level with no pointer, and a
 search reads one line per level, i.e.,
 \f[
   H = 1 + \Big\lceil \log_{B+1} \Big\lceil \frac{n}{B} \Big\rceil
       \Big\rceil \tag{5}
 \f]
 lines, against the \f$ \log_2 n - \log_2 B \f$ misses of the binary search; in
 a node the child to take is the count of the keys smaller than \f$ v \f$,
 which the vectors of the processor compute with no branch (`countLess()` of
 `CountLess.h`).

 The PGM-index of Ferragina and Vinciguerra (`PGMIndex`) replaces the inner
 nodes by a model of the keys: the points \f$ ( A[ i ] , i ) \f$ are covered by
 the fewest segments whose lines predict each place within \f$ \epsilon \f$,
 and the first keys of the segments are covered in turn, with error
 \f$ \epsilon_r \f$, up to a single segment. The fewest segments are found in
 one pass by the algorithm of O'Rourke, which keeps the lines that pass between
 the points moved up and down by \f$ \epsilon \f$ as the region between two
 convex hulls, each point updating them in amortized \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. Since a
 horizontal line through the middle of any \f$ 2 \epsilon \f$ consecutive
 places is within the error, a segment covers at least \f$ 2 \epsilon \f$ keys;
 hence the last level has \f$ m \le n / ( 2 \epsilon ) \f$ segments, there are
 \f$ O( \log_{2 \epsilon_r} m ) \f$ levels, and a search, a binary search of
 \f$ O( \epsilon_r ) \f$ places per level and one of \f$ O( \epsilon ) \f$
 places at the end, costs
 \f[
   O( \log \epsilon_r \cdot \log_{2 \epsilon_r} m + \log \epsilon ) , \tag{6}
 \f]
 in a space proportional to \f$ m \f$, which on regular keys is far smaller
 than \f$ n / ( 2 \epsilon ) \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The searches laid out for the memory</caption>
 <tr><th>Structure</th><th>Lines read</th><th>Space besides
     \f$ A \f$</th><th>Source</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>branchlessLowerBound()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \lceil \log_2 n \rceil + 1 \f$ probes, misses overlapped</td>
     <td>none</td><td>Khuong and Morin (2017)</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SPlusTree</tt></td><td>\f$ H \f$ of (5)</td>
     <td>\f$ n / B \f$ items</td><td>Slotin (2022)</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PGMIndex</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log_{2 \epsilon_r} m ) \f$ windows,
         \f$ O( \epsilon / B + 1 ) \f$ lines at the end</td>
     <td>28 bytes per segment</td>
     <td>Ferragina and Vinciguerra (2020)</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_search_minimum The minimum and the local optima

 <em>Lemma 2 (the minimum).</em> Every comparison-based algorithm that finds
 the minimum of \f$ n \f$ distinct items makes \f$ n - 1 \f$ comparisons at
 least (section 2.6), and `min()` of `Minimum.h` makes exactly \f$ n - 1 \f$
 (section 1.2).

 Indeed, an item that has never lost a comparison may still be the minimum, and
 a comparison makes at most one item lose for the first time; hence
 \f$ n - 1 \f$ comparisons are needed to leave a single item that never lost.
 The same argument gives the bound of the minimum and the maximum together
 (`minMax()`), where an item must lose once to be excluded from being the
 minimum and win once to be excluded from being the maximum: a comparison of
 two items never compared gives one loss and one win at once, the others at
 most one of the two, and pairing the items before comparing them with the
 current minimum and maximum spends
 \f[
   \Big\lceil \frac{3 n}{2} \Big\rceil - 2 \tag{7}
 \f]
 comparisons, which is optimal (Pohl, beyond the book); the divide et impera of
 `minMaxDivide()` reaches (7) when \f$ n \f$ is a power of 2, by the recurrence
 \f$ T( n ) = 2 T( n / 2 ) + 2 \f$, \f$ T( 2 ) = 1 \f$.

 A local optimum is cheaper than the minimum. A <em>peak</em> of \f$ A \f$ is
 an item not smaller than its neighbours, the places out of \f$ A \f$ counting
 as \f$ -\infty \f$, and one always exists, e.g., the maximum. `findPeak()` of
 `LocalOptima.h` keeps the invariant that
 \f$ A[ \mathit{lo} .. \mathit{hi} ] \f$ rises into \f$ \mathit{lo} \f$ and
 falls out of \f$ \mathit{hi} \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ A[ \mathit{lo} - 1 ] < A[ \mathit{lo} ] \f$ and
 \f$ A[ \mathit{hi} ] \ge A[ \mathit{hi} + 1 ] \f$: under it the maximum of the
 part is a peak of \f$ A \f$, and comparing \f$ A[ m ] \f$ with
 \f$ A[ m + 1 ] \f$ keeps it on one of the two halves. Hence a peak costs
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$ comparisons, against the \f$ n - 1 \f$ of the
 maximum. On a binary tree the walk down to a smaller child gives a local
 minimum in \f$ O( h ) \f$, \f$ h \f$ the height; on general graphs no such
 bound exists, since a local minimum of the hypercube of dimension \f$ d \f$
 needs about \f$ 2^{d / 2} \f$ evaluations (Aldous).

 \section theory_search_select The selection

 The item of rank \f$ k \f$ can be found without sorting. The selection of
 Hoare (`selection()` of `Selection.h`, section 17.1) partitions the part
 around a pivot as quicksort does, and goes on in the side that holds rank
 \f$ k \f$ only. With the pivot chosen at random its rank is uniform, the side
 kept is in the worst case the larger one, and the expected number of
 comparisons satisfies
 \f[
   T( n ) \le n - 1 + \frac{2}{n} \sum_{q = \lfloor n / 2 \rfloor}^{n - 1}
   T( q ) , \tag{8}
 \f]
 whose solution, by substitution of \f$ T( q ) \le c q \f$, is
 \f$ T( n ) \le c n \f$ with \f$ c = 4 \f$; hence the selection is linear in
 expectation on every input, while a pivot that is always the smallest item
 gives \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the median of the medians).</em> If the pivot is the median of
 the medians of the groups of 5 items, at least \f$ 3 n / 10 - O( 1 ) \f$ items
 lie on each side of it, and the selection makes
 \f[
   T( n ) \le T( n / 5 ) + T( 7 n / 10 ) + O( n ) = O( n ) \tag{9}
 \f]
 comparisons in the worst case (Blum, Floyd, Pratt, Rivest and Tarjan).

 Indeed, half of the \f$ n / 5 \f$ medians are not larger than the pivot, and
 each of them has 3 items of its group not larger than it; the same holds on
 the other side. The pivot costs a selection on \f$ n / 5 \f$ items, the
 remaining part has at most \f$ 7 n / 10 \f$ of them, and since
 \f$ 1 / 5 + 7 / 10 < 1 \f$ the work of the levels of the recursion falls
 geometrically, whence (9) (`medianOfMedians()`).

 The engineered `nthElement()` of `NthElement.h` reaches fewer comparisons than
 (8) by the sampling of Floyd and Rivest: a sample of about \f$ n^{2/3} \f$
 items, selected recursively, gives a pivot whose rank is just past \f$ k \f$
 with high probability, so that one partition leaves the part of rank \f$ k \f$
 with \f$ o( n ) \f$ items, and the expected number of comparisons is
 \f$ n + \min( k , n - k ) + o( n ) \f$ (Kiwiel), which is optimal up to the
 term \f$ o( n ) \f$. It keeps the bound (9) in the worst case by falling back
 to the median of the medians after too many partitions that cut little.

 <table>
 <caption>The selection of the item of rank \f$ k \f$</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Pivot</th><th>Comparisons</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>selection()</tt></td><td>at random, or the first item</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ expected by (8), \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ in the worst
         case</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>medianOfMedians()</tt></td><td>the median of the medians of
     the groups of 5</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ in the worst case, by (9)</td>
     </tr>
 <tr><td><tt>nthElement()</tt></td><td>the sample of Floyd and Rivest,
     the median of three on short parts</td>
     <td>\f$ n + \min( k , n - k ) + o( n ) \f$ expected,
         \f$ O( n ) \f$ in the worst case</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_search_rmq Range minima

 After a preprocessing of \f$ A \f$, the query \f$ \mathrm{RMQ}( i , j ) \f$ is
 answered without scanning the range. The <em>sparse table</em> (`SparseTable`
 of `RangeMinimum.h`) stores \f$ \mathrm{RMQ}( i , i + 2^L - 1 ) \f$ for every
 \f$ i \f$ and \f$ L \f$, each from two entries of the row before, in
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$; a query takes the better of the two entries of length
 \f$ 2^L \f$, \f$ L = \lfloor \log_2 ( j - i + 1 ) \rfloor \f$, that start at
 \f$ i \f$ and end at \f$ j \f$, which cover the range, since the minimum does
 not mind the overlap.

 The linear preprocessing rests on a tree. The <em>Cartesian tree</em> of
 \f$ A \f$ has the leftmost minimum of \f$ A \f$ as its root, and the Cartesian
 trees of the parts on its left and on its right as its subtrees
 (`cartesianTree()`, which builds it in \f$ O( n ) \f$ with a stack holding its
 right spine, each item pushed and popped once).

 <em>Lemma 4 (Bender and Farach-Colton).</em> \f$ \mathrm{RMQ}( i , j ) \f$ is
 the lowest common ancestor of \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$ in the Cartesian tree of
 \f$ A \f$, i.e., the node of least depth among those that the Euler tour of
 the tree visits between the first visits of \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$.

 Indeed, the lowest common ancestor \f$ a \f$ of \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$ is the
 first node whose place falls in \f$ [ i , j ] \f$ on the path from the root,
 so that the range lies in the subtree of \f$ a \f$, whose root is its minimum;
 between the two first visits the tour does not leave that subtree and returns
 to \f$ a \f$, the only node of least depth there. The depths along a tour
 differ by exactly 1 at each step, and the queries on such an array
 (`PlusMinusOneRangeMinimum`) are answered in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ after
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ preprocessing: the array is cut into blocks of
 \f$ d = \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor / 2 \f$ items, a sparse table on the
 \f$ n / d \f$ minima of the blocks costs
 \f$ O( ( n / d ) \log n ) = O( n ) \f$, and a block is determined, up to an
 additive constant, by its \f$ d - 1 \f$ steps of \f$ \pm 1 \f$; hence the
 answers inside all the possible blocks are built once in a table (the trick of
 the Four Russians) of
 \f[
   2^{d - 1} \cdot d^2 \le \sqrt{n} \, \log_2^2 n = o( n ) \tag{10}
 \f]
 entries. Thus `RangeMinimum` answers in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ after \f$ O( n ) \f$
 preprocessing on any array.

 In practice the constant factors of the reduction are large, and
 `WordRangeMinimum.h` keeps the decomposition into blocks but answers inside a
 block of 64 items by the bits of a word: the stack of the minima from the
 right of the block up to \f$ j \f$, as a mask of 64 bits, has as its lowest
 bit at or above \f$ i \f$ the leftmost minimum of \f$ A[ i .. j ] \f$, one
 count of the trailing zeros, and a query makes at most five reads of the
 memory, independent of one another.

